# Prefix `not` as the operand of `==` hides a chained comparison (M1-19).
mode: syntax
---- query
from T | where a == not b == c
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..31
  PIPELINE@0..30
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    NAME_REF@5..6
      IDENT@5..6 "T"
    WHITESPACE@6..7 " "
    PIPE@7..8 "|"
    WHITESPACE@8..9 " "
    WHERE_OP@9..30
      IDENT@9..14 "where"
      WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
      BIN_EXPR@15..30
        NAME_REF@15..16
          IDENT@15..16 "a"
        WHITESPACE@16..17 " "
        EQ2@17..19 "=="
        WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
        PREFIX_EXPR@20..30
          NOT_KW@20..23 "not"
          WHITESPACE@23..24 " "
          BIN_EXPR@24..30
            NAME_REF@24..25
              IDENT@24..25 "b"
            WHITESPACE@25..26 " "
            EQ2@26..28 "=="
            WHITESPACE@28..29 " "
            NAME_REF@29..30
              IDENT@29..30 "c"
  WHITESPACE@30..31 "\n"

error[DS0114]: `not` needs parentheses here
  --> query:1:21
  |
1 | from T | where a == not b == c
  |                     ^^^ `not` binds more loosely than `==`
  |
  = help: write it as a call to say what it negates: `not(b == c)`
  = note: without parentheses, `not` negates everything up to the next `and` or `or`
